    overview:
      "Welcome to French domestic policy. This is the structural mirror image of the German system covered elsewhere in this course: instead of sixteen Länder holding genuine constitutional authority, France centralizes domestic policy in the national government, a unitary state whose constitution even draws a formal line around exactly what a law may cover at all. Over the next forty minutes you will work through ten ideas in the order a real French domestic policy fight actually encounters them. We start with the unitary state itself and the constitutional division between the domain of statute and the domain of executive regulation that shapes what parliament even needs to be involved in, before turning to the technocratic elite that designs policy options across successive governments and the constitutional council that formally consults civil society and economic actors before a bill is drafted. We then turn to how the French public itself can intervene directly, through the referendum tradition and, more recently, through deliberative bodies like the Citizens' Convention on Climate, and to how organized pressure outside any formal institution, strikes and street protest, functions as its own genuinely institutionalized check. We close with the executive's own toolkit for moving policy without full parliamentary negotiation, ordonnances and the more famous Article 49.3, and with the decentralization reforms that have, since the 1980s, given France's regions real if still modest autonomy within this fundamentally centralized structure. Keep one question running underneath everything you hear: is this a matter parliament actually has to vote on at all, and if it does, does the government actually have the votes, or does it need one of this system's other tools instead.",
