        whyItMatters:
          "This significantly reduces the importance of large-scale private fundraising as a campaign strategy lever compared to systems with more permissive spending rules, since French campaigns compete more on message discipline, media presence, and organizational reach within firm, legally binding spending ceilings than on raw financial resource advantage. You should connect this directly back to the parrainage threshold: both mechanisms are part of the same broader French regulatory instinct to structure presidential competition through formal, codified thresholds rather than leaving access and resource advantage to pure market dynamics.",
        example:
          "A French presidential candidate cannot simply outspend opponents through unlimited fundraising the way is possible in less-regulated systems, since hitting the legal spending cap is a real, binding constraint every serious campaign has to plan carefully around well before election day itself. Nicolas Sarkozy's 2012 campaign was later found by the Conseil constitutionnel to have exceeded the legal spending ceiling, a finding with real consequences, reduced public reimbursement and a subsequent criminal prosecution, that illustrates just how binding the cap actually is in practice rather than a purely notional limit.",
