        whyItMatters:
          "Anticipating whether and how strongly a republican front will materialize is a genuine, high-stakes strategic calculation for both the far-right candidate, hoping it weakens, and their second-round opponent, hoping it holds, and it is a real, actively contested political dynamic rather than an automatic or guaranteed outcome. You should connect this directly back to the two-round structure this lecture opened with, since the republican front is precisely the second-round persuasion problem in its starkest form, an entire election decided not by a candidate's own first-round coalition but by how much of the rest of the electorate is willing to vote against, rather than for, the finalist they actually oppose.",
        example:
          "Marine Le Pen's two subsequent runoff losses, to Macron in both 2017 and 2022, showed a still-real but visibly weaker republican-front effect than 2002, with her second-round vote share climbing from under 18% in 2002's equivalent position to over 41% in 2022, evidence the dynamic constrains rather than eliminates far-right advancement. The 2024 snap legislative elections saw a modernized version of the same logic applied constituency by constituency, with candidates from other blocs withdrawing from three-way run-off races specifically to consolidate an anti-National Rally vote, a direct legislative-level descendant of the presidential-level front républicain tradition.",
