    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring test: which round is this campaign decision actually built for, and does it still make sense once the electorate voting in the other round is the one that matters. The two-round system and the parrainages threshold both shape who even gets to compete and how, one determines eventual victory, the other determines eligibility to start, while campaign finance limits and temps de parole rules constrain how campaigns compete once underway, keeping the contest more about message than resources. The legislative-election calendar sequencing determined what kind of parliamentary support a winning president could once expect automatically, and front républicain dynamics are the recurring strategic wildcard shaping second-round outcomes whenever a far-right candidate advances, with vote blanc and vote nul totals as the visible residue of voters the front républicain logic never fully persuades. The collapse of the traditional governing parties is what turned a stable two-party alternation into today's fragmented three-bloc field, and the legislative coalition strategy that has emerged since 2022 is the direct downstream consequence, a genuinely new, coalition-aware legislative campaign logic built for a National Assembly majority that can no longer simply be assumed. As you follow any real French campaign from here forward, try running it through all ten ideas in order, and always ask which round, and which of these three blocs, the tactic you are watching is actually built for.",
